--TEST--
A `Core\Heap<T>` drained by `pop` and the same table given to `Core\Arr::sort` answer the same sequence under every ordering both of them accept — natural, reversed, keyed and bytewise — docs/spec/01-core-library.md §§ 2 and 9, `rule:classes/comparable`
--FILE--
<?nvs
// Two members implement one rule: `nvs_stdlib::ordering::compare_values` is
// what a comparator-less `Core\Heap` sifts by and what `Core\Arr::sort`
// permutes by, and a user callable is threaded into both. So the question
// worth asking is not what either one printed — the neighbouring cases read
// a drained heap and a sorted array off their own lines already — but whether
// the two **agree**, position for position. A member that grew its own
// comparison still looks right on its own line and fails here.
//
// Values, not positions: `-1` appears twice, so the permutation is not
// unique, but the sequence of values is. Comparing what came out is
// therefore the assertion that survives a tie broken either way.
final class Agree {
    // Pushes the whole table, then walks the sorted answer popping once per
    // entry, and counts the positions where the two say the same thing. A
    // heap that drained short throws at `pop` rather than scoring low, which
    // is `heap-holds-every-push-and-refuses-an-empty-read.nvst`'s rule.
    public static function ints(Core\Heap<int> $h, array<int> $table, array<int> $sorted): int {
        foreach ($table as int $v) {
            $h->push($v);
        }
        int $agreed = 0;
        foreach ($sorted as int $want) {
            int $got = $h->pop();
            if ($got == $want) {
                $agreed = $agreed + 1;
            }
        }
        return $agreed;
    }

    public static function words(Core\Heap<string> $h, array<string> $table, array<string> $sorted): int {
        foreach ($table as string $t) {
            $h->push($t);
        }
        int $agreed = 0;
        foreach ($sorted as string $want) {
            string $got = $h->pop();
            if ($got == $want) {
                $agreed = $agreed + 1;
            }
        }
        return $agreed;
    }
}

// One rule written twice: the heap takes it as a comparator, `sort` takes it
// as a `{by}` key function. Neither spelling may reorder the table
// differently from the other.
final class Key {
    public static function magnitude(int $v): int {
        if ($v < 0) {
            return 0 - $v;
        }
        return $v;
    }
}

// A duplicate, a negative and a zero, none of them in order.
array<int> $table = [5, -1, 3, -1, 0, 9];

// 1. The natural order, with no comparator anywhere: both members reach
// `compare_values` and nothing else.
var $natural = new Core\Heap<int>();
echo "natural ", Agree::ints($natural, $table, Core\Arr::sort($table)), " of 6\n";

// 2. Reversed. The heap says it with a comparator; `sort` says it with
// `rule:types/array-combination`'s `{order}` option and a `Core\Order` case, so the two do not even
// share a spelling — only an answer.
var $reversed = new Core\Heap<int>(fn (int $a, int $b): int => $b - $a);
echo "reversed ", Agree::ints($reversed, $table, Core\Arr::sort($table, {order: Core\Order::Desc})), " of 6\n";

// 3. A key neither member computes itself. `magnitude` is chosen so no two
// entries key alike — a tie here would make the value sequence ambiguous
// again and the count would stop meaning anything.
array<int> $spread = [-5, 2, -1, 4, -3];
var $keyed = new Core\Heap<int>(fn (int $a, int $b): int => Key::magnitude($a) - Key::magnitude($b));
echo "keyed ", Agree::ints($keyed, $spread, Core\Arr::sort($spread, {by: fn (int $v): int => Key::magnitude($v)})), " of 5\n";

// 4. Strings, where `compare_values` is bytewise and case therefore sorts
// before letters — the divergence from PHP's numeric-string comparison that
// `Core\Arr::sort`'s own doc comment records. Both members take it or
// neither does.
array<string> $words = ["pear", "Fig", "apple", "fig"];
var $lex = new Core\Heap<string>();
echo "bytewise ", Agree::words($lex, $words, Core\Arr::sort($words)), " of 4\n";

// Every heap drained to nothing: the agreement above is over whole
// sequences, not over a prefix that happened to line up.
echo "drained ", $natural->count(), $reversed->count(), $keyed->count(), $lex->count(), "\n";

// The counter can say no. Draining a max-heap against the ascending answer
// is the same code over a genuinely different sequence, and it agrees
// nowhere — so a run that scores 6 above is scoring agreement rather than
// counting the loop.
var $crossed = new Core\Heap<int>(fn (int $a, int $b): int => $b - $a);
echo "crossed ", Agree::ints($crossed, $table, Core\Arr::sort($table)), " of 6\n";
--EXPECT--
natural 6 of 6
reversed 6 of 6
keyed 5 of 5
bytewise 4 of 4
drained 0000
crossed 0 of 6
